\begin_layout Title
Postulados
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Jhordan Silveira de Borba
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
There is no general consensus as to what its fundamental principles are,
 how it should be taught,
 or what it really “means.” Every competent physicist can “do” quantum mechanics,
 but the stories we tell ourselves about what we are doing are as various as the tales of Scheherazade,
 and almost as implausible (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "griffiths2017introduction"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Analogy with Classical Mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Newton's Second Law
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A law of physics can be understood as a rule that describes—
usually by means of a mathematical formulation—
how certain physical systems behave(
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Siddiqui2014"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Newton's second law,
 for example,
 can be expressed by the equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F\left(x,t\right)=m\frac{d^{2}}{dt^{2}}x\left(t\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the position of a particle of mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 relative to the origin at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 When we solve this differential equation under certain initial conditions,
 we obtain as a solution a function 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 once the forces acting on the particle and its initial conditions are specified,
 Newton's second law determines its temporal evolution:
 solving the equation provides us with the equation of motion of the particle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 for a free particle(
\begin_inset Formula $F=0$
\end_inset

),
 we can obtain its equation of motion from Newton's second law:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0=m\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining the velocity as 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)\equiv\frac{dx}{dt}$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
0 & =\frac{d^{2}x}{dt^{2}}\\
0 & =\frac{dv}{dt}\\
0\cdot\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt' & =\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dv}{dt'}dt'\\
0 & =v\left(t\right)-v\left(t_{0}\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 
\begin_inset Formula $v\left(t\right)=v\left(t_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 the particle's velocity is constant.
 Denoting this constant velocity by 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
v\left(t\right) & =v\left(t_{0}\right)\\
\frac{dx}{dt} & =v\\
\int^{t}_{t_{0}}\frac{dx}{dt'}dt' & =v\int^{t}_{t_{0}}dt'\\
x\left(t\right)-x\left(t_{0}\right) & =v\left(t-t_{0}\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t_{0}\right)=x_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{0}=0$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x\left(t\right)=x_{0}+vt
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is easily interpretable as the position of the particle at the instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Schrödinger Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The quantum mechanical analogue of Newton's second law is the Schrödinger equation.
 In one dimension and in the non-relativistic case,
 the Schrödinger equation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi\left(x,t\right)=-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\Psi\left(x,t\right)+V\left(x,t\right)\Psi\left(x,t\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corresponding equation for a free particle (
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(x,t\right)=0$
\end_inset

) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}\Psi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Using the method of separation of variables,
 that is,
 assuming that the solution can be separated into a spatial part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and a temporal part 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right) & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x^{2}}\left(\Phi\left(x\right)\Pi\left(t\right)\right)\\
i\hbar\frac{1}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m}\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since one side depends only on the spatial dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

) and the other only on the temporal dimension (
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

),
 the only way this can be true is if both sides are equal to a constant:
 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

.
 Solving then for the temporal part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{i\hbar}{\Pi}\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial t} & =E\\
\frac{d\Pi}{dt} & =-\frac{iE}{\hbar}\Pi
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the derivative of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

 is equal to the function itself multiplied by a constant,
 we have an exponential solution 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\left(t\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For the spatial part:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\Phi}\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\\
\frac{\partial^{2}\Phi}{\partial x^{2}} & =-\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}\Phi
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since this is a second-order linear homogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients,
 we can look for solutions of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x\right)=e^{\pm ikx}$
\end_inset

.
 In this case,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{ikx}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-ikx}$
\end_inset

 are two linearly independent solutions to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{d^{2}\Phi}{dx^{2}}=-k^{2}\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 By the linearity of the equation,
 its general solution is a linear combination of these solutions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Psi\left(x,t\right)=\Pi\left(t\right)\Phi\left(x\right)=A\exp\left(-\frac{iE}{\hbar}t\right)\left(B\exp\left(-i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)+C\exp\left(i\sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}}x\right)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Or,
 writing 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{2}=2mE/\hbar^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega=\frac{E}{\hbar}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\Psi\left(x,t\right) & =K_{1}e^{-i\left(kx+\omega t\right)}+K_{2}e^{i\left(kx-\omega t\right)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{2}$
\end_inset

 are constants determined by the conditions imposed on the solution.
 Here,
 some questions already arise.
 When we solved Newton's second law,
 we obtained 
\begin_inset Formula $x\left(t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which described the position of the particle.
 When we solve the Schrödinger equation,
 we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

,
 but what is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

?
 What does 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\left(x,t\right)$
\end_inset

 describe?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A somewhat more in-depth discussion of this can be found 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "here"
target "/dupla"
type "other"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Postulates of quantum mechanics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To move forward,
 we need to know the postulates
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A postulate is a proposition assumed to be true within a theory,
 serving as a starting point for the construction of its concepts and results.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of quantum mechanics.
 The first point to note is that they can be formulated in different ways from one book to another.
 We will use Professor Jonas Maziero's material (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 We need to begin,
 then,
 with some comments on Dirac notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this notation,
 we write a vector as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 called a ket.
 Taking the conjugate transpose of this vector (or adjoint),
 we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle v\right|=\left|v\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left(\left|v\right\rangle ^{T}\right)^{*}$
\end_inset

,
 which is called a bra.
 If we represent the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

,
 we can write it as a linear combination of the basis vectors:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|v\right\rangle =\sum_{i}\left\langle b_{i}|v\right\rangle \left|b_{i}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 In matrix representation,
 we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The product of a bra and a ket is called the inner product and results in a scalar:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle w|v\right\rangle =\left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}\left|v\right\rangle =\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]=\sum^{n}_{j=1}\left\langle b_{j}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\left\langle b_{j}|v\right\rangle 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

On the other hand,
 the product of a ket and a bra is called the outer product and results in an operator,
 whose matrix representation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|v\right\rangle \left\langle w\right|=\left|v\right\rangle \left|w\right\rangle ^{\dagger}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \\
\vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle 
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{1}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots\\
\left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{1}|w\right\rangle ^{*} & \dots & \left\langle b_{n}|v\right\rangle \left\langle b_{n}|w\right\rangle ^{*}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With this in mind,
 we can now move on to the postulates of quantum mechanics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
State Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
In every physical theory,
 we seek to describe the possible states of the system of interest,
 the changes in these states (temporal evolution),
 and the observable quantities and their measurements.
 For example,
 in Newtonian Mechanics (NM),
 the state of a system at a certain instant of time is specified by the values of position and velocity relative to a certain inertial reference frame,
 and its temporal evolution is given by Newton's second law.
 The observables are essentially the positions,
 from which we obtain the velocities using the time parameter.
 It is implicit in NM that the values of the observables are determined even before they are measured,
 and that all these observables can have well-defined values at a given instant of time.
 Next,
 we will see the postulates of QM for describing states,
 changes in these states,
 observables,
 and their measurements.
 We will see that the measurement part differs greatly from that of NM,
 mainly because of the existence of observables that cannot have well-defined values at the same time (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "maziero"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The state of a quantum system is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 For simplicity,
 we will consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 as a vector space equipped with an inner product function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \cdot|\cdot\right\rangle :\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 a Hilbert space is a complete complex vector space.
 To understand what it means to be complete,
 we first need to understand the concept of a Cauchy sequence.
 A Cauchy sequence is a sequence whose elements become arbitrarily close to one another as we advance through the sequence.
 A complete metric space
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A metric space is,
 essentially,
 a set of objects in which we define a way of measuring the distance between two objects.
 In the case of a vector space with an inner product,
 the distance can be defined from the norm induced by the inner product.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is one in which every Cauchy sequence converges to a limit that also belongs to the space itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By system,
 we refer to a degree of freedom
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A degree of freedom corresponds to an independent physical variable that can vary and that can be used to characterize the state of a system.
 For example,
 the position and spin of a particle correspond to different degrees of freedom.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 For example,
 the spin of an electron is a system,
 another system would be its position,
 and so on.
 When we consider two or more degrees of freedom as a single entity,
 we call it a composite system,
 and if we divide the composite system into subsystems,
 its state is described by a normalized vector in a Hilbert space obtained by taking the tensor product of the individual spaces:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{a}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{b}\otimes\dots
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Measurement Postulate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observable (measurable) quantities are represented by linear Hermitian operators,
 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

,
 defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 When we measure an observable,
 we will obtain one of its possible values,
 which are given by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike classical mechanics (CM),
 quantum mechanics (QM),
 in general,
 does not allow us to predict with certainty the outcome of a measurement.
 What QM provides us with are the probabilities of obtaining the different outcomes,
 calculated by means of the Born rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P\left(o|\psi\right)=\left|\left\langle \psi|o\right\rangle \right|^{2}=\left|\left\langle o|\psi\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the probability of obtaining the value 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

 when measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 of a system that is in the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 immediately before the measurement,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|o\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 is the eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $O=O^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 are defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 When we measure an observable,
 we obtain one of its possible values,
 which are represented by the eigenvalues of the Hermitian operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before moving forward,
 we can stop to reflect for a moment.
 When we were working with classical mechanics and Newton's law,
 one of the observables of a particle was its position,
 and Newton's law directly described how this quantity varied in time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 when we approach quantum mechanics,
 the Schrödinger equation describes how the wave function varies in time (and space).
 With Dirac notation,
 as we will see when studying the dynamics postulate,
 we have a mathematical description of how the state vector of the system varies in time.
 However,
 the state vector is not itself an observable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Even temporarily ignoring the probabilistic aspect,
 we use the state vector to calculate quantities associated with observables.
 This already constitutes a fundamental difference between the classical and quantum descriptions of systems.
 But if we calculate scalar quantities associated with observables from the state vector (or the wave function) of the system,
 what is the physical meaning of the state vector itself?
 Does it even have a direct physical meaning?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is one of the points on which different interpretations of quantum mechanics diverge.
 There is still no consensus on which interpretation provides the correct description of physical reality,
 although some interpretations may be ruled out or considered problematic in light of certain theoretical arguments and experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Likewise,
 different interpretations diverge on the question of whether all relevant physical information about a system is necessarily contained in its state vector.
 We will explore different interpretations later on.
 For now,
 it is enough to emphasize that the physical meaning of the state vector and the status of the information it contains are profound interpretational questions on which there is no consensus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Repeatability of measurements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 there is one more detail that,
 although it is not explicitly contained in the postulates we have presented so far,
 is significantly different when we are in the quantum domain:
 performing a measurement affects the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we observe the position of a soccer ball that moves according to classical mechanics,
 we can,
 in principle,
 make this observation without significantly altering the state of the ball.
 We can therefore consider that the observation was performed approximately “externally” to the system itself,
 leaving it practically unchanged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the quantum domain,
 however,
 the situation is different.
 When we perform a measurement,
 the system interacts with the measurement apparatus and,
 in general,
 its state is significantly altered.
 One consequence of this is that,
 if we measure the same observable twice immediately in succession,
 the result of the second measurement will necessarily be equal to that obtained in the first.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Indeed,
 suppose that the first measurement of the observable (
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

) produced the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
 After this measurement,
 the state of the system becomes a state associated with the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
 In the case where the eigenvalue (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

) is non-degenerate
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A non-degenerate eigenvalue is one to which a single eigenvector corresponds,
 up to a global phase,
 that is,
 a complex phase factor 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}$
\end_inset

 that multiplies the state vector and does not affect the measurement outcomes,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\phi}\left(e^{i\phi}\right)^{*}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 we can write
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(o|\psi\right)=1,\quad\rightarrow\left|\psi\right\rangle =e^{i\phi}\left|o\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A second immediately subsequent measurement of the same observable will therefore once again produce the result (
\begin_inset Formula $o$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that,
 theoretically,
 before the measurement is performed,
 quantum mechanics provides us only with the probabilities associated with the possible outcomes.
 In the laboratory,
 however,
 an individual realization of the measurement necessarily produces a definite outcome.
 This leads us to another fundamental question:
 the probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics.
 Its probabilistic predictions concern the frequencies and distributions of outcomes obtained from ensembles of systems prepared in equivalent ways and subjected to the same experimental procedure,
 and they do not,
 in general,
 determine which specific outcome will be obtained in an individual realization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before the measurement is performed,
 we have only the probabilities associated with the different values we may obtain.
 In this situation,
 in what state is the system?
 When dealing with classical mechanics,
 deterministic dynamics allows us,
 in principle,
 to determine the state of a system at an earlier instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t'<t$
\end_inset

 by knowing its state at an instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Can we extend this line of reasoning to quantum mechanics?
 Is the probabilistic character of the theory a fundamental feature of nature,
 or merely an expression of our ignorance about the state of the system?
 Again,
 we have no conclusive answers to this question,
 and different interpretations of quantum mechanics offer different answers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And finally,
 still related to the probabilistic nature of measurements,
 misguided interpretations often support the idea that quantum mechanics is a scientific formulation that naturally incorporates the possibility of free will.
 However,
 the probabilistic character of the theory,
 by itself,
 provides no support for this conclusion and does not make quantum mechanics compatible with free will.
 We can discuss this misconception in greater detail when we address the dynamics postulate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Quantum incompatibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 be two Hermitian operators defined on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space of dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

.
 By the spectral theorem,
 we can write them in their respective spectral decompositions as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right| & B= & \sum^{d}_{j=1}b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle b_{j}\right|
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{j}$
\end_inset

 are the respective eigenvalues,
 and the sets
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|b_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d}_{j=1}$
\end_inset

 form orthonormal bases of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 respectively,
 that is,
 they diagonalize these operators,
 so that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & =a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle  & B\left|b_{j}\right\rangle = & b_{j}\left|b_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that the two operators share the same basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =\left|b_{j}\right\rangle \coloneqq\left|j\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 then,
 with the commutator defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset

,
 we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left[A,B\right] & =\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)-\left(\sum^{d}_{k=1}b_{k}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\right)\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j|k\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle k|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}a_{j}b_{k}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle k\right|\delta_{jk}-\sum^{d}_{j,k=1}b_{k}a_{j}\left|k\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\delta_{jk}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\left(a_{j}b_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|-b_{j}a_{j}\left|j\right\rangle \left\langle j\right|\right)\\
 & =0
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0=\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA\Longrightarrow AB=BA
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 we have that if the operators share the same set of eigenvectors,
 they commute.
 And to prove that if they commute,
 they share the same basis of eigenvectors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
A\left(B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right) & =\left(AB\right)\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \\
 & =B\left(A\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =B\left(a_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =a_{j}B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 in this case,
 we must have that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 belong to the eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate,
 its eigenspace has dimension 1.
 Therefore,
 any eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is linearly dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 also belongs to this eigenspace,
 we necessarily have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 that is,
 if there are two or more linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to the same eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 then there exists a subspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 generated by the eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 It is a little more complex,
 but it is the same idea applied to a subspace of dimension greater than 1.
 To understand this,
 we will base our discussion on another source (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "MIT804_CommonEigenbases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the degenerate case,
 we then have more vectors associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us call this subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} ^{d_{j}}_{k=1}$
\end_inset

 being any basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =a_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 that is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms vectors from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 into vectors that also belong to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 transforms an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 into a vector that remains in the same eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \neq0$
\end_inset

,
 this vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 each 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 can be written as a linear combination of the basis vectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we can consider the action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 “internally” within the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian,
 its restriction to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also Hermitian.
 And since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 has finite dimension (and is Hermitian),
 we can diagonalize this restriction of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 there exists a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 which we can denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle \right\} $
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle =b_{j}\left|a^{k}_{j}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Since this new basis remains a basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 its vectors continue to be eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the eigenvectors are simultaneously eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 consisting of common eigenvectors of the two operators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 if the Hermitian operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 then they share the same orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.
 To better understand this proof,
 we can represent the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a matrix in the basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Using the completeness relation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j}\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{j}\right|$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\mathbb{I}B\mathbb{I}=\sum_{j,k}\left(\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \right)\left(\left|a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{k}\right|\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 the elements of the matrix representing 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 in this basis are 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{a_{j},a_{k}}=\left\langle a_{j}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 or in matrix form:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{1}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{1}\right\rangle  & \left\langle a_{2}\left|B\right|a_{2}\right\rangle  & \dots\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we can think of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 as a block-diagonal matrix:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
B_{1} & 0 & 0\\
0 & B_{2} & 0\\
0 & 0 & \ddots
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where each 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is a submatrix representing the internal action of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 acting on the subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-degenerate,
 then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is just a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times1$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 then the block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{j}\times d_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian,
 each block 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{j}$
\end_inset

 is also a Hermitian matrix and,
 therefore,
 can be diagonalized.
 Thus,
 we can diagonalize 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 within each subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 obtaining a basis of eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we prove that if and only if two operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 then they share a common basis of eigenvectors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Example 1"
target "/exemplo-incompatibilidade"
type "other"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary,
 when we have a non-degenerate eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 the situation is very simple,
 since in this case,
 the only eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 up to a multiplicative factor,
 is automatically also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

 is degenerate,
 its eigenvectors form a subspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 in which every nonzero vector is also an eigenvector of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 commute,
 applying 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 to any vector in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 results in another vector belonging to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 it is sufficient to find,
 within this subspace,
 the vectors that are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This possibility is guaranteed by the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian:
 its action restricted to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 can be represented by a Hermitian submatrix,
 which can be diagonalized by an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The fact that a matrix is Hermitian guarantees that we can choose,
 in the space on which it acts,
 an orthonormal basis formed by its eigenvectors (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "DelftSpectralTheorem"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "on this page"
target "https://interactivetextbooks.tudelft.nl/mqp-v2/operators.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 we can choose an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 formed by eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Since all vectors in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{j}$
\end_inset

 are also eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 these vectors are common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Repeating this procedure for each eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain an orthonormal basis of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 formed by common eigenvectors of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the proof of this theorem,
 we know that if two observables 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of a system do not commute,
 they cannot share all eigenvectors.
 If,
 by measuring the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 we prepare the system in a state that is not an eigenvector of the observable 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 this vector must necessarily be a linear combination of the eigenvectors of with two or more nonzero probability amplitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Example 2"
target "/exemplo-incompatibilidade"
type "other"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Relação de Incerteza
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aqui usaremos o formalismo cinemático quântico para obter relações matemáticas gerais que fornecem limitações para o produto ou soma das 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

incertezas
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (desvios padrões) sobre dois observáveis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Relação de incerteza de Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger (RIHRS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A RIHRS lê-se:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right)\ge(\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right))^{2}+2^{-2}|\langle[A,B]\rangle|^{2},
\]

\end_inset

com a variância definida por 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}(A)=\langle(A-\langle A\rangle)^{2}\rangle.$
\end_inset

 As quantidades no lado direito da desigualdade serão definidas a seguir.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Para provar a RIHRS,
 usaremos como uma das principais ferramentas a desigualdade de Cauchy-Scharz (DCS):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\psi|\psi\rangle\langle\phi|\phi\rangle\ge|\langle\psi|\phi\rangle|^{2}=\langle\psi|\phi\rangle\langle\phi|\psi\rangle,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

com a igualdade obtida se e somente se 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 são colineares (
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle\propto|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

).
 Esse resultado é válido para qualquer par de vetores 
\begin_inset Formula $|\psi\rangle,|\phi\rangle$
\end_inset

 em um espaço de Hilbert 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considere 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 dois observáveis de um sistema físico preparado no estado 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi\rangle$
\end_inset

.
 Usaremos 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\langle X\rangle:=\langle X\rangle_{\xi}=\langle\xi|X|\xi\rangle
\]

\end_inset

para denotar o valor médio de um operador qualquer 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 para denotar o operador identidade em 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

.
 Antes de prosseguirmos,
 gostaria de exlorar mais esse resultado.
 Vamos considerar um sistema no estado 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\psi\right\rangle =\sum_{n}c_{n}\left|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 e um operador 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 com autovalores 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}$
\end_inset

 associados aos autovetores 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|x_{n}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

.
 Pela definição de média ponderada,
 o valor médio dos resultados é 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}P\left(o_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 como 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(o_{n}\right)=\left|c_{n}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 podemos escrever 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|c_{n}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Como 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle =c_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 então 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle O\right\rangle =\sum_{n}o_{n}\left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 uma vez que 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle \right|=\sqrt{\left\langle o_{n}|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \psi|o_{n}\right\rangle }$
\end_inset

.
 Então pela decomposição espectral 
\begin_inset Formula $O=\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}\right|$
\end_inset

 então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle O\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\right|\left(\sum_{n}o_{n}\left|o_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle o_{n}\right|\right)\left|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\left|o\right|\psi\right\rangle 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Vamos definir ainda os seguintes vetores:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
|\psi\rangle & =(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,\\
|\phi\rangle & =(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,
\end{align}

\end_inset

que substituiremos na DCS.
 Notemos que 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\langle\psi|\psi\rangle & =\left((A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle\right)\\
 & =\left(|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{\dagger}(A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle\right)\\
 & =\langle\xi|(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{2}|\xi\rangle\\
 & =\mathrm{Var}(A),\\
\langle\phi|\phi\rangle & =\mathrm{Var}(B),
\end{align}

\end_inset

com 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}(X)=\mathrm{Var}(X)_{\xi}=\langle\xi|(X-\langle X\rangle I)^{2}|\xi\rangle=\langle(X-\langle X\rangle I)^{2}\rangle$
\end_inset

 sendo a variância de 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Também usamos 
\begin_inset Formula $(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{\dagger}(A-\langle A\rangle I)=(A-\langle A\rangle I)^{2}$
\end_inset

 uma vez que 
\begin_inset Formula $A=A^{\dagger}$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Podemos ver que isso decorre de do fato de 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 (e 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

) serem hermitianos uma vez que:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*} & =\left(\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle \right)^{*}=\left\langle \psi\left|A^{\dagger}\right|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle =\left\langle A\right\rangle 
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ademais,
 temos que 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\langle\psi|\phi\rangle & =((A-\langle A\rangle I)|\xi\rangle,(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =(|\xi\rangle,(A-\langle A\rangle I)(B-\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =(|\xi\rangle,(AB-A\langle B\rangle-\langle A\rangle B+\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle I)|\xi\rangle)\\
 & =\left\langle \xi\left|\left(AB-A\langle B\rangle-\langle A\rangle B+\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle I\right)\right|\xi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle \xi\left|AB\right|\xi\right\rangle -\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|B\right|\xi\right\rangle -\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|B\right|\xi\right\rangle +\left\langle \xi\left|A\right|\xi\right\rangle \left\langle \xi\left|BI\right|\xi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle +\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Sendo:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left[A,B\right] & =AB-BA,\\
\left\{ A,B\right\}  & =AB+BA
\end{align}

\end_inset

são,
 respectivamente,
 o comutador e anti-comutador de 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Somando:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left[A,B\right]+\left\{ A,B\right\}  & =AB-BA+AB+BA\\
 & =2AB\\
2^{-1}\left(\left[A,B\right]+\left\{ A,B\right\} \right) & =AB
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
AB=2^{-1}(\{A,B\}+[A,B]).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle  & =\left\langle AB\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle 2^{-1}(\{A,B\}+[A,B])\right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \\
 & =2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \{A,B\}\right\rangle +\left\langle [A,B]\right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Até aqui temos então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \psi|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi|\phi\right\rangle  & \ge\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\left|2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \{A,B\}\right\rangle +\left\langle [A,B]\right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Como 
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]$
\end_inset

 são,
 respectivamente,
 operadores Hermitianos (
\begin_inset Formula $\{A,B\}^{\dagger}=\{A,B\}$
\end_inset

) e anti-Hermitianos (
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]^{\dagger}=-[A,B]$
\end_inset

),
 seus valores médios são,
 respectivamente,
 números reais e imaginários puros.
 Que o valor médio de um hermitiano é um número real já verificamos,
 agora sobre um anti-hermitiano:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*} & =\left(\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle \right)^{*}=\left\langle \psi\left|A^{\dagger}\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\left\langle \psi\left|A\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\left\langle A\right\rangle 
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agora se 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =a+ib$
\end_inset

,
 seu conjugado 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}=a-ib$
\end_inset

,
 se 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle ^{*}=-\left\langle A\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 então 
\begin_inset Formula $a+ib=-\left(a-ib\right)$
\end_inset

,
 ou seja 
\begin_inset Formula $a+ib=-a+ib$
\end_inset

,
 pra isso ser igual então 
\begin_inset Formula $a=0$
\end_inset

.
 e ficamos apenas com 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle =ib$
\end_inset

.
 Como 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle A\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 também são números reais.
 Podemos verificar sobre 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\} $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left(A,B\right) & =AB\mp BA\\
\left(A,B\right)^{\dag} & =\left(AB\mp BA\right)^{\dagger}\\
 & =\left(AB\right)^{\dagger}\mp\left(BA\right)^{\dagger}\\
 & =B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}\mp A^{\dagger}B^{\dagger}\\
 & =\mp A^{\dagger}B^{\dagger}+B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ou seja,
 se 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]=AB-BA$
\end_inset

 então 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[A,B\right]^{\dagger}=-\left[A,B\right]$
\end_inset

.
 E se 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\} =AB+BA$
\end_inset

 então 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A,B\right\} ^{\dagger}=\left\{ A,B\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Então podemos escrever:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle  & =\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)+i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\\
 & =\left[2^{-1}\left(\left\langle \left\{ A,B\right\} \right\rangle \right)-\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle \right]+\left[2^{-1}\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ou seja:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\Re(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle) & =2^{-1}\langle\{A,B\}-2\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle\mathbb{I}\rangle,\\
\Im(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle) & =-i2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Obs.:
 Como 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle=ib$
\end_inset

 é um nummero puramente imaginário,
 fazendo 
\begin_inset Formula $-i\cdot\left(ib\right)=b$
\end_inset

 obtemos seu coeficiente real.
 Assim,
 considerando que
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|^{2} & =\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle ^{*}\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \\
 & =\left(\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)-i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\right)\left(\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)+i\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)\right)\\
 & =\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Temos então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}(A)\mathrm{Var}(B) & \geq(\Re(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle))^{2}+(\Im(\langle\psi|\phi\rangle))^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agora vamos abrir:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=\left(-i2^{-1}\langle[A,B]\rangle\right)^{2}=-2^{-2}\langle[A,B]\rangle^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Além disso como é puramente imginário então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\langle[A,B]\rangle^{2}=\left(-ib\right)^{2}=-b^{2}=-\left|b\right|^{2}=-\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ficamos então com 
\begin_inset Formula $\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 E nossa desigualdade:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle \psi|\psi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi|\phi\right\rangle  & \ge\left|\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right|{}^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+\Im\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nos resta agora demonstrar que:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=CovQ\left(A,B\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{Cov\left(A,B\right)+Cov\left(BA\right)}{2}\right)^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Onde a covariância é 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)=\langle XY\rangle-\langle X\rangle\langle Y\rangle$
\end_inset

 entre os observáveis 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

,
 logo:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2} & =\left(\frac{\langle AB\rangle-\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle+\langle BA\rangle-\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle}{2}\right)^{2}\\
\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2} & =\left(\frac{\langle AB\rangle+\langle BA\rangle-2\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle}{2}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left(\frac{\left\langle AB+BA\right\rangle }{2}-\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left(2^{-1}\left\langle \left\{ AB\right\} \right\rangle -\langle B\rangle\langle A\rangle\right)^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E este 
\begin_inset Formula $\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)=2^{-1}\left\langle \left\{ A,B\right\} \right\rangle -\left\langle A\right\rangle \left\langle B\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 é o resultado que tínhamos obtido.
 Podemos escrever então 
\begin_inset Formula $\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}=\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

 e:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\Re\left(\left\langle \psi|\phi\right\rangle \right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}\\
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Vale observar que se 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 são observáveis compatíveis,
 i.e.,
 se 
\begin_inset Formula $[A,B]=0$
\end_inset

,
 teremos que 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)=\mathrm{Cov}(A,B)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Vamos analizar agora o problema da possível trivialidade da RIHRS,
 que tem por objetivo geral delimitar o quão pequeno pode ser o produto das variâncias de um par de observáveis.
 Para isso vamos assumir que o sistema está preparado em um estado que é autovetor do observável 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|\xi\rangle=|a\rangle
\]

\end_inset

com 
\begin_inset Formula $A|a\rangle=a|a\rangle$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Neste caso pode-se verificar que 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Var}(A)_{|a\rangle}=\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)_{|a\rangle}=\langle[A,B]\rangle_{|a\rangle}=0.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

E com isso a RIHRS nos retorna
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0\mathrm{Var}(B)\ge0.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Por conseguinte,
 neste caso,
 a RIHRS não fornece informação alguma sobre a possível incompatibilidade de 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 e 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 O mesmo tipo de limitação se aplica se 
\begin_inset Formula $|\xi\rangle=|b\rangle$
\end_inset

 para 
\begin_inset Formula $B|b\rangle=b|b\rangle.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
De toda forma,
 vamos verificar.
 Para o comutador:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\left\langle a_{k}\left|\left[A,B\right]\right|a_{k}\right\rangle  & =\left\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \\
 & =\left\langle a_{k}\left|\sum_{n}a_{n}\left|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\right|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\sum_{n}a_{n}\left|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\right|\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \\
 & =\sum_{n}a_{n}\left[\left\langle a_{k}|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{n}\right\rangle \left\langle a_{n}|a_{k}\right\rangle \right]\\
 & =a_{k}\left[\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle \right]\\
 & =0
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Para a variância,
 vamos adotar outra estratégia,
 ao invés de escrever a decomposição espectral,
 vamos usar que 
\begin_inset Formula $A|a\rangle=a|a\rangle$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Var}(A)_{|a\rangle} & =\langle a_{n}|(A-\langle a_{n}\left|A\right|a_{n}\rangle I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|(A-a_{n}\langle a_{n}|a_{n}\rangle I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|(A-a_{n}I)^{2}|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =\langle a_{n}|A^{2}|a_{n}\rangle+a^{2}_{n}\langle a_{n}|a_{n}\rangle-2a_{n}\langle a_{n}|A|a_{n}\rangle\\
 & =a^{2}_{n}+a^{2}_{n}-2a^{2}_{n}\\
 & =0
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E para a Covariância Quântica,
 já vimos que 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle a_{n}\left|A\right|a_{n}\rangle=a_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\right\rangle =\left\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\right\rangle =a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle $
\end_inset

,
 então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{CovQ}(A,B)_{|a\rangle} & =\frac{\langle a_{k}\left|AB\right|a_{k}\rangle+\langle a_{k}\left|BA\right|a_{k}\rangle-2\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\rangle\langle a_{k}\left|A\right|a_{k}\rangle}{2}\\
 & =\frac{a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle +a_{k}\left\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\right\rangle -2a_{k}\langle a_{k}\left|B\right|a_{k}\rangle}{2}\\
 & =0
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Relações de incerteza de Robertson e de Heisenberg (RIH)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ignorando-se
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Isto é,
 se 
\begin_inset Formula $a\geq b+c\geq c$
\end_inset

,
 sendo 
\begin_inset Formula $b\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 então necessariamente 
\begin_inset Formula $a\geq c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 o termo relacionado à covariância quântica na RIHRS e tomando a raiz quadrada da expressão que resta,
 obtemos a relação de incerteza de Robertson:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\text{Var}\left(A\right)\text{Var}\left(B\right) & \geq\text{CovQ}\left(A,B\right)^{2}+2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}\\
\sqrt{\text{Var}\left(A\right)}\sqrt{\text{Var}\left(B\right)} & \geq\sqrt{2^{-2}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|^{2}}\\
\sigma_{A}\sigma_{B} & \ge2^{-1}\left|\left\langle \left[A,B\right]\right\rangle \right|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
com e.g.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{A}=\sqrt{Var(A)}.$
\end_inset

 Quando aplicada a posição e momento linear é uma das relações de incerteza de Heisenberg:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{X}\sigma_{P}\ge2^{-1}|\langle[X,P]\rangle|=\hbar/2,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

pois 
\begin_inset Formula $[X,P]=i\hbar I$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aqui estamos em condições de realizar mais algumas discussões.
 A relação de incerteza de Heisenberg é constantemente fruto de más compreensões.
 A primeira delas diz respeito ao próprio status da relação.
 Embora seja tradicionalmente chamada de princípio de incerteza,
 ela não precisa ser postulada como um princípio.
 Como vimos,
 a relação de Heisenberg pode ser obtida como um caso particular da Relação de incerteza de Heisenberg-Robertson-Schrödinger,
 que,
 por sua vez,
 pode ser derivada da desigualdade de Cauchy–Schwarz,
 juntamente com as propriedades matemáticas dos observáveis representados por operadores em um espaço de Hilbert.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
O segundo ponto é que é incorreto interpretar a relação de incerteza como afirmando que uma partícula 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

não tem posição e momento definidos
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 ou que 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

não podemos medir posição e momento na mesma preparação
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 A primeira afirmação é uma interpretação possível,
 mas não é o conteúdo matemático da relação,
 enquanto a segunda é simplesmente incorreta.
 A relação de incerteza não diz que é impossível obter resultados de posição e momento na mesma preparação;
 ela estabelece uma relação entre as variâncias dos resultados dessas medidas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Em outras palavras,
 podemos preparar repetidamente o sistema no mesmo estado e realizar medidas de posição e de momento.
 A partir dos resultados,
 podemos determinar as respectivas distribuições e,
 consequentemente,
 suas variâncias.
 A relação de incerteza estabelece que o produto dessas variâncias —
 ou,
 mais precisamente,
 dos desvios-padrão —
 deve satisfazer uma dada relação.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Isso contrasta com a mecânica clássica.
 Podemos imaginar,
 por exemplo,
 uma partícula parada em uma posição perfeitamente determinada.
 Ignorando limitações experimentais,
 seria possível conceber um estado clássico no qual a posição e a velocidade fossem simultaneamente bem definidas,
 por exemplo,
 uma particula parada na origem (
\begin_inset Formula $x=0,v=0$
\end_inset

).
 Nesse caso,
 se repetíssemos as medidas,
 obteríamos sempre os mesmos valores e,
 portanto,
 teríamos 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}=\sigma_{v}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Postulado da dinâmica
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As mudanças de estados em MQ são descritas por uma transformação unitária:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
|\psi_{t}\rangle=U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=U_{t}U^{\dagger}_{t}=\mathbb{I}.
\]

\end_inset

O operador de evolução temporal sendo obtido da equação de Schrödinger:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

com a condição inicial 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{0}=\mathbb{I},
\]

\end_inset

onde 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}$
\end_inset

 é o operador Hamiltoniano do sistema no instante de tempo 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar$
\end_inset

 é a constante de Planck dividida por 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 e usamos a notação 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{t}=\frac{\partial}{\partial_{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 As duas equações acima são equivalentes à equação de Schrödinger para o estado 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle=H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Verificamos esse resultado como segue:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle & =i\hbar\partial_{t}(U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle)\\
 & =(i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t})|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}U_{t}|\psi_{0}\rangle\\
 & =H_{t}|\psi_{t}\rangle.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Exemplo
\series default
:
 Quando 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{t}=H$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nesse tipo de situação,
 i.e.,
 quando o Hamiltoniano não depende do tempo,
 podemos verificar que a seguinte forma geral é válida:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Faremos isso primeiramente usando a decomposição espectral do Hamiltoniano,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H=\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|,
\]

\end_inset

e aplicando a função de operador:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}\right)\\
 & =i\hbar\partial_{t}\exp\left(\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aqui devemos fazer uma pausa para entender a matemática.
 Vimos que para um operador hermitiano qualquer 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 temos 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(T)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}T^{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Primeiro podemos ver que 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{2}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
H^{2} & =\left(\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\left(\sum_{n}E_{n}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|\right)=\sum_{j,n}E_{j}E_{n}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\rangle\langle E_{n}|=\sum_{j}E^{2}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Onde usamos o fato de que 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle E_{j}|E_{n}\right\rangle =\delta_{jn}$
\end_inset

.
 De modo geral temos que 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{n}=\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

.
 Escrevendo então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\exp(H) & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}H^{n}\\
 & =\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{1}{n!}\left(\sum_{j}E^{n}_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)\\
 & =\sum_{j}\left(\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{E^{n}_{j}}{n!}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
E sendo 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(x)=\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}$
\end_inset

,
 então:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(H)=\sum_{j}e^{E_{j}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|$
\end_inset

.
 No nosso caso temos então:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t} & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\partial_{t}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\sum_{j}\left(-\frac{iE_{j}}{\hbar}\right)\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =i\hbar\left(-\frac{i}{\hbar}\right)\sum_{j}\exp\left(-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}\right)E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\\
 & =\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}H|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}\\
 & =H\left(\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|\right)
\end{align}

\end_inset

em que usamos 
\begin_inset Formula $H|E_{j}\rangle=E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle$
\end_inset

 ao longo de toda demonstração.
 as agora sendo então que 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{j}e^{-\frac{iE_{j}t}{\hbar}}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|=e^{-\frac{it}{\hbar}\sum_{j}E_{j}|E_{j}\rangle\langle E_{j}|}=e^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Então 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=He^{-\frac{iHt}{\hbar}|}$
\end_inset

 e como 
\begin_inset Formula $i\hbar\partial_{t}U_{t}=H_{t}U_{t},$
\end_inset

logo 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}=e^{-iHt/\hbar}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OBS.
 Note que poderíamos obter 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{t}$
\end_inset

 resolvendo a equação de Schrödinger para cada hamiltoniano 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

.
 No entanto,
 tendo essa fórmula geral,
 podemos começar a resolução já calculando a exponencial,
 sem precisar resolver a equação diferencial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agora podemos abordar uma outra questão que se relaciona ao “determinismo” como oposição ao livre-arbítrio.
 Há pelo menos dois sentidos para a palavra determinismo.
 Um deles é mais associado à física clássica e consiste na ideia de que,
 se conhecemos perfeitamente o estado atual de um sistema e as leis que governam sua evolução,
 seu estado futuro e passado são,
 em princípio,
 determinados.
 O outro é um sentido mais amplo,
 de natureza filosófica,
 que diz respeito à negação do livre-arbítrio.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Quanto ao primeiro sentido,
 a formulação usual da mecânica quântica introduz um elemento probabilístico que contrasta com o determinismo da mecânica clássica.
 Os resultados das medidas não são,
 em geral,
 determinados de maneira unívoca pelo estado quântico anterior,
 mas descritos por distribuições de probabilidade.
 Nesse sentido,
 a teoria apresenta um elemento indeterminista.
 Vale notar,
 contudo,
 que a equação de Schrödinger que governa a evolução do estado quântico é determinista.
 Dado o estado inicial e o Hamiltoniano do sistema,
 a equação determina univocamente o estado quântico em qualquer instante posterior.
 As probabilidades dos resultados de uma medida são então obtidas a partir desse estado,
 por meio da regra de Born.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Se a natureza é,
 em última instância,
 determinista e a natureza probabilística da mecânica quântica decorre de alguma descrição incompleta,
 ou se a indeterminação quântica é fundamental,
 é uma questão para a qual não possuímos uma resposta consensual.
 Diferentes posicionamentos a respeito dessa questão dão origem,
 em parte,
 a diferentes interpretações da mecânica quântica.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Porém,
 é um equívoco pensar que esse elemento matemático probabilístico serviria,
 por si só,
 como suporte para uma defesa do livre-arbítrio.
 Não há,
 nas leis físicas conhecidas,
 uma formulação que incorpore o livre-arbítrio.
 Podemos aproximar a chance de obter cara ou coroa ao lançar uma moeda de maneira aproximadamente simétrica a 50% para cada resultado.
 Esse é um evento probabilístico,
 mas não diríamos,
 por isso,
 que a moeda possui livre-arbítrio.
 A introdução de probabilidades na descrição da natureza,
 portanto,
 não confere mais livre-arbítrio à natureza do que o lançamento probabilístico de uma moeda.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Além disso,
 não apenas a evolução do estado quântico é determinista,
 como a própria distribuição de probabilidade em qualquer instante impõe restrições aos possíveis resultados e às suas probabilidades.
 Ao meu ver,
 essas restrições são incompatíveis com uma concepção de “livre” arbítrio na qual o agente deveria possuir liberdade irrestrita para determinar qualquer um dos resultados possíveis.
 
\end_layout

